// Additional architecture chapters grounded in documents present in the
// supplied Chromium checkout. Implementation paths absent from that sparse
// checkout are marked research cited in each chapter's source list.
export function systems(source) {
  return {
    startup: {
      id:'startup', part:'Browser architecture', title:'How Chromium starts its processes',
      deck:'Follow the executable from its entry point to browser and renderer work.',
      deps:['owners'], skill:'Process startup and initialization',
      lead:'Before a renderer can parse a page, some process has to create it, choose its role, initialize shared libraries, and establish its connection to the browser. Startup teaches a useful distinction: a process is an operating-system container, while the role it runs is a decision made during initialization.',
      flow:['Platform entry point','Common initialization','Choose process role','Set up role','Run event loop'],
      sections:[
        ['One executable, several process roles',`<p>The checked-in <code>docs/design/startup.md</code> describes Chrome as mostly one executable used for several process types. A platform entry point reaches common initialization and then dispatches according to the internal <code>--process-type</code> switch. The browser process is the outer coordinator; a renderer process runs page code under different privileges. That distinction is established by launch and initialization, not by giving every role a separate product binary.</p><p>This document is an overview and contains older platform notes. Treat its general dispatch model as a starting hypothesis, then inspect the current <code>chrome/app</code> entry point and <code>content/app</code> process dispatch at the revision under study before asserting an exact call stack. Use the document to find the relevant code, then check the call path before relying on its details.</p>`],
        ['Why initialization has to be ordered',`<p>Common setup must happen before role-specific work can rely on it: command-line parsing, logging, locale and resource initialization, feature configuration, and IPC bootstrap each have consumers. Role-specific setup then decides which threads, services, and sandbox restrictions that process needs. Doing expensive or privileged initialization in every child wastes resources and may violate a process’s security model.</p><p>To investigate a startup failure, ask which phase was reached, which process type failed, and whether the required service was initialized before first use. Record the process command line and the first failing log or stack frame. A crash in a renderer entry path can look like a generic browser launch failure if you only observe the window disappearing.</p>`],
        ['Who creates a renderer',`<p>The browser decides when work needs a renderer and which site or frame it should host. The <code>RenderProcessHost</code> is browser-side process management; <code>RenderFrameHost</code> represents browser-side frame state; renderer-side objects receive the committed document and construct the page. Those names describe separate lifetimes. A new navigation may reuse a renderer, choose another one, or fail before a renderer is ready.</p><p>Draw two columns for a real investigation. In the browser column put the process host, frame host, and navigation request. In the renderer column put the main thread, Blink frame, and document. Add a line only when you find the IPC or launch call that creates the relationship. This prevents a common mistake: assuming a browser-side object physically lives in the child process it manages.</p>`],
        ['Startup and sandboxing constrain each other',`<p>A child needs some resources before restrictive policy is applied, but the point of creating a separate process is to confine untrusted work. Chromium’s sandbox design calls the privileged controller a broker and the restricted child a target. The browser starts the target and defines the policy; the exact mechanisms differ across Windows, Linux, macOS, and other platforms.</p><p>That means “move this initialization into the renderer” is a security and lifecycle proposal, not just a code motion. Ask whether the operation needs a file, socket, device, or privileged handle. If it does, inspect whether a browser-side service can provide a narrow capability instead. Read <code>docs/design/sandbox.md</code> alongside the platform-specific process-sandbox table before relying on one platform’s launch behavior.</p>`],
        ['Trace a startup claim to code',`<p>Start at <code>docs/design/startup.md</code>, find the platform entry point it names, follow common initialization, and locate process-type dispatch. Then pick one renderer-only initialization and one browser-only initialization. For each, write the function, source path, thread or sequence, and failure behavior. A simple call graph is more useful than a long list of symbols if it shows when the role becomes known.</p><p>If a named implementation file is missing from this sparse checkout, use the pinned upstream link and record the revision. Do not infer that an old design note still describes the current launch order in every detail. The broad model—common startup followed by role-specific work—is separate from a current function’s exact name.</p>`],
        ['Engineering lesson: explicit phase boundaries',`<p>Startup bugs often involve hidden ordering: component B assumes A has initialized, but the call path for one process type skips A. The general technique is to make phase transitions visible. Find where a property first becomes true, list consumers that rely on it, and write a focused test or startup trace for the unusual process type. The same technique applies to shutdown, navigation commit, and asynchronous service launch.</p><p>Before changing an initialization step, predict three consequences: which process types execute it, whether the sandbox permits it at that point, and how the browser behaves if it fails. Then verify those predictions in source or a run.</p>`]
      ],
      quiz:['A browser-side RenderProcessHost exists. Does that prove the renderer has already initialized Blink?', 'No. A process host is browser-side management state. Find the launch and renderer initialization/handshake points before claiming the child is ready.'],
      lab:{title:'Map one renderer launch',aim:'Identify the transition from browser decision to running renderer.',prediction:'The browser-side process host and renderer-side entry point have distinct initialization paths.',steps:['Read docs/design/startup.md and docs/process_model_and_site_isolation.md.','Find the current process-type dispatch in chrome/app or content/app at the pinned revision.','Follow the path that creates a renderer and identify where the sandbox is applied.','Draw the browser and renderer columns with the first IPC connecting them.'],observation:'A browser object may exist before the child is ready to execute page code.',conclusion:'Process ownership, readiness, and document ownership are separate states.',follow:'What happens if the renderer dies during this startup interval?'},
      files:[source('docs/design/startup.md','Entry points and process dispatch'),source('docs/process_model_and_site_isolation.md','When a renderer is needed'),source('docs/security/process-sandboxes-by-platform.md','Sandbox roles by platform'),source('chrome/app/chrome_main.cc','Common startup path','src',false),source('content/app/content_main.cc','Content process dispatch','src',false)]
    },
    ipc: {
      id:'ipc', part:'Browser architecture', title:'How Chromium processes communicate',
      deck:'Understand Mojo pipes, generated interfaces, binding, replies, and trust.',
      deps:['startup','owners'], skill:'IPC contracts and asynchronous failure',
      lead:'A renderer cannot simply call a browser-process C++ object. Mojo gives each side a typed endpoint and transports messages between them. The interesting engineering work is deciding who may obtain an endpoint, what a message means, how long it stays valid, and what happens if the peer disappears.',
      flow:['Declare .mojom','Create pipe','Transfer endpoint','Bind receiver','Dispatch task','Reply or disconnect'],
      sections:[
        ['A message pipe is the basic transport',`<p><code>docs/mojo_and_services.md</code> defines a message pipe as two endpoints, each with an incoming queue. A <code>.mojom</code> file describes an interface and message types; generated bindings turn that description into C++ types. A <code>mojo::Remote</code> sends interface calls. A <code>mojo::Receiver</code> dispatches them to an implementation. A call with a response sends another message back. The apparent method call is therefore an asynchronous protocol, not a shared stack.</p><p>A useful model is a pair of mailboxes with a typed contract. The contract says which messages are well formed. It does not automatically prove that a less privileged sender is authorized to request the operation. Message parsing, authorization, sequence order, and object lifetime are separate questions.</p>`],
        ['Follow a receiver from creation to implementation',`<p>The documentation’s example creates a <code>Remote</code>, calls <code>BindNewPipeAndPassReceiver()</code>, and transfers a <code>PendingReceiver</code> to the browser. The pending endpoint is just a typed handle until the browser binds it to a <code>Receiver</code> and implementation. A received message is dispatched as scheduled work on the receiving sequence. To follow a real interface, search for its <code>.mojom</code>, then its binder registration, receiver field, implementation method, and tests.</p><p>For frame-scoped requests, <code>BrowserInterfaceBroker</code> gives a renderer frame a way to request approved interfaces. The binder map in <code>content/browser/browser_interface_binders.cc</code> connects the requested interface to a browser-side host. Asking for an interface and being allowed to use it are both part of the architecture.</p>`],
        ['Scope the endpoint to the right owner',`<p>A frame-scoped interface can use the browser’s knowledge of the corresponding <code>RenderFrameHost</code>. Worker brokers are separate; the checked-in guide names dedicated, shared, and service-worker hosts. A process-level broker is broader and requires thread-safe handling. Choose the narrowest scope that expresses the real authority of the operation.</p><p>Imagine an API that reads data for the current document’s origin. If the endpoint survives the document that obtained it, a later document could inherit authority by accident. Locate who owns the <code>Receiver</code> and when it disconnects. Then check whether the implementation uses browser-side committed origin rather than an origin string supplied in the message. An IPC design is also a lifetime and authorization design.</p>`],
        ['Ordering is local to a connection',`<p>A message pipe gives a useful ordering relationship along that pipe. It does not magically order all tasks in two processes. Chromium also has navigation-associated interfaces for cases where messages need to be ordered with navigation traffic; the documentation describes their shared FIFO path. If you observe “message B arrived before navigation A,” identify the pipes and queues involved before assuming a violated contract.</p><p>Write a two-process sequence diagram for a real call. Include the task that sends, the receiving queue, the implementation method, and any reply. Mark where another navigation, disconnect, or posted task can interleave. This diagram often reveals a race that a single-process call stack hides.</p>`],
        ['Replies, disconnects, and tests',`<p>The sender must keep its <code>Remote</code> alive if it expects a reply. The receiver must remain bound long enough to process the request. Either peer can disappear: renderer crash, navigation teardown, service shutdown, or explicit reset. A robust caller needs to specify whether a callback is dropped, receives an error, or has a disconnect handler. Never infer success merely because the call was sent.</p><p><code>docs/mojo_testing.md</code> shows how to bind a fake receiver and use <code>base::test::TestFuture</code> to wait for an asynchronous result. A fake verifies the caller’s behavior under a chosen response; it does not verify the production service. Add an integration test when the binder, authorization, or process boundary is the behavior under study.</p>`],
        ['The security review starts at the receiver',`<p><code>docs/security/mojo.md</code> says policy should remain in the browser and data from a less privileged process must be validated. Prefer a focused operation over a method with many optional fields and ambiguous states. If a renderer sends an origin, URL, size, or permission claim, ask whether the browser can derive it from trusted frame state instead. Where it cannot, validate the value against that state.</p><p>The important failure is not merely malformed serialization. A perfectly valid message can ask for an unauthorized operation. Trace where the implementation turns message data into filesystem, network, storage, or UI authority. That is the point at which a typed interface becomes a security boundary.</p>`],
        ['An investigation template for any Mojo API',`<p>Write six lines before you edit the interface: <b>sender</b> and its trust level; <b>receiver</b> and sequence; <b>pipe acquisition</b>; <b>lifetime</b>; <b>authoritative data</b>; <b>failure response</b>. Then read the <code>.mojom</code>, generated binding use, binder registration, implementation, and one test. If any line remains unknown, identify the exact source search or runtime trace that would answer it.</p><p>This method generalizes to RPC in distributed systems: typed messages are only the beginning. The contract must also define authority, ordering, retries, and failure.</p>`]
      ],
      quiz:['A renderer sends a well-typed origin field over Mojo. Is it safe for the browser to authorize storage from that field?', 'No. The browser should use trusted frame or process state, or validate the claimed origin against it. A valid message can still be an unauthorized request.'],
      lab:{title:'Trace one browser interface',aim:'Find every step between a renderer request and a browser implementation.',prediction:'A .mojom declaration alone will not identify the binder, owner, or security check.',steps:['Read docs/mojo_and_services.md through the BrowserInterfaceBroker example.','Choose one frame interface from content/browser/browser_interface_binders.cc at the pinned revision.','Find its .mojom, binder registration, receiver owner, implementation, and test.','Draw the message and reply path; mark what a renderer crash or navigation does to the pipe.'],observation:'The binder and receiver lifecycle supply facts the interface declaration cannot.',conclusion:'IPC behavior is a protocol plus ownership and authorization.',follow:'Can the receiver derive every privileged fact from browser-side state?'},
      files:[source('docs/mojo_and_services.md','Message pipes and interface brokers'),source('docs/mojo_testing.md','Testing asynchronous interfaces'),source('docs/security/mojo.md','IPC design and validation'),source('cc/mojom/render_frame_metadata.mojom','Concrete .mojom example'),source('content/browser/browser_interface_binders.cc','Frame and worker binder registration','src',false),source('third_party/blink/public/mojom/browser_interface_broker.mojom','Interface acquisition contract','src',false)]
    },
    network: {
      id:'network', part:'Browser architecture', title:'How Chromium loads documents and resources',
      deck:'Separate navigation, response policy, subresource loading, and network failures.',
      deps:['navigation','ipc','security'], skill:'Network request and response reasoning',
      lead:'A page load is not one fetch. The browser manages a navigation, a response may come from several sources, the renderer later requests scripts and images, and policy checks decide which bytes may cross into an untrusted process. The source available here supports the boundaries and debugging method; the network implementation itself is sparse.',
      flow:['Start request','Choose loader/factory','Receive headers','Apply response policy','Commit document','Load subresources'],
      sections:[
        ['Navigation requests and subresource requests differ',`<p><code>docs/navigation.md</code> separates navigation from loading. The browser coordinates the request that may create a new document. After commit, Blink parses that document and discovers subresources such as scripts, styles, and images. A subresource failure therefore may occur after the navigation has succeeded. Debugging “the page did not load” starts by identifying which phase and which request failed.</p><p>Make a request table with initiator, frame, target URL, redirect chain, response headers, and result. Include the document request and at least one script or image. Do not treat the address bar URL as evidence that every resource was fetched from the same site or by the same process.</p>`],
        ['The response may not come from the wire',`<p>The navigation document explicitly notes alternatives to an external network fetch: a service worker, WebUI, cache, and special URLs such as <code>data:</code>. Therefore “network request” often means a request through a loader abstraction, not proof that a socket was used. When checking behavior, identify the loader or interceptor that supplied the response. In DevTools, compare a normal reload, cache disabled, and a service-worker-controlled page.</p><p>This distinction is a broader systems lesson: an interface can hide several implementations, but their semantics may differ at failure and caching boundaries. Find the point where the implementation chooses one path before attributing a result to DNS, HTTP, or the service worker.</p>`],
        ['Headers decide what can commit',`<p>After redirects, headers tell Chromium whether the result is a new document, a download, no-content response, or an error page. <code>docs/navigation.md</code> names 204/205 and <code>Content-Disposition</code> as cases that leave the old document active. It also describes response-driven process selection and security context: for example, a CSP sandbox header can give the document an opaque origin. Commit occurs only after the chosen renderer creates the document and acknowledges it to the browser.</p><p>For a 204 response, predict the browser’s committed document and history state before testing. If the address bar changes temporarily while the old page remains visible, distinguish pending URL from last-committed origin. The same technique prevents security decisions based on display state.</p>`],
        ['A URLLoaderFactory is a capability',`<p><code>docs/security/compromised-renderers.md</code> says only the privileged browser should create the <code>network::mojom::URLLoaderFactory</code> objects that handle HTTP requests. Its parameters can restrict request initiator origin and other policy-sensitive behavior. A renderer can ask a factory to load something, but the browser controls what kind of factory it receives.</p><p>That architecture limits a compromised renderer: it cannot simply fabricate a more permissive factory by sending a plausible URL. To analyze a request, trace factory creation, its parameters, the renderer’s call, and the response checks before bytes reach the renderer. The exact implementation lives under <code>services/network</code> and related content code, absent from this sparse checkout; inspect pinned upstream for detailed call paths.</p>`],
        ['Response filtering and cross-origin data',`<p>Some cross-origin responses are valid subresources; others contain sensitive data a renderer must not read. The compromised-renderer threat model discusses cross-origin read blocking and browser-controlled factories as defenses. The important question is not only whether a server answered. It is whether this requester, in this context, may receive the headers and body.</p><p>For a blocked request, record the initiator origin, target origin, resource type, credentials, response headers, and the policy that rejected or filtered it. Reproducing the same URL in a top-level tab is not the same test as loading it as a cross-origin subresource. The request context is part of the input.</p>`],
        ['Traffic annotations record intent',`<p><code>docs/network_traffic_annotations.md</code> requires annotations near the code that initiates network communication. They describe the sender, purpose, data sent, and controls available to the user or administrator. This is static documentation carried through network APIs; it is not metadata added to every packet at runtime.</p><p>The annotation makes the request’s purpose reviewable. When investigating an unexpected background request, search its annotation and caller. Compare the declared intent with the runtime trace. An annotation cannot prove the request is safe, but it gives a concrete claim that source review and experiments can check.</p>`],
        ['Debug a load in phases',`<p>Use a small decision tree. Did navigation start? Did a loader produce headers? Did redirects settle? Did response policy allow commit? Did the browser receive the renderer’s commit acknowledgement? Did parsing discover the missing subresource? Did that subresource get bytes? Did rendering display them? Each answer sends you to a different directory and test.</p><p>Capture a trace or NetLog and pair events with frame and request identifiers. Then inspect the source path for the first phase whose observed state differs from your prediction. This is more efficient than searching for a class named after the final visual symptom.</p>`]
      ],
      quiz:['A page commits successfully but its script fails to load. Should you start with NavigationRequest commit logic?', 'First check the script subresource request and loader path. A successful document commit does not imply every later subresource succeeds.'],
      lab:{title:'Separate document and subresource loading',aim:'Locate the first failing phase of a controlled page load.',prediction:'A 204 navigation leaves the current document active; a missing subresource can fail after a successful commit.',steps:['Read the Network Request, Commit, and Loading sections of docs/navigation.md.','Use a small local page with one script or image and inspect requests in DevTools.','Try a document response with no content or a failed subresource; record which document remains active.','Locate the browser-side URLLoaderFactory creation and the renderer-side resource request in pinned source.'],observation:'Navigation state and subresource status can disagree without contradiction.',conclusion:'A page load is a sequence of separately owned operations.',follow:'Which policy check would stop a compromised renderer from reading a cross-origin response?'},
      files:[source('docs/navigation.md','Navigation versus loading'),source('docs/security/compromised-renderers.md','Request and response trust boundary'),source('docs/network_traffic_annotations.md','Network request intent'),source('services/network/public/mojom/url_loader_factory.mojom','Loader factory protocol','src',false),source('content/browser/loader/','Browser loader implementation','src',false),source('third_party/blink/renderer/platform/loader/fetch/resource_fetcher.cc','Blink subresource scheduling','src',false)]
    },
    security: {
      id:'security', part:'Browser architecture', title:'How Chromium keeps web content isolated',
      deck:'Reason about origins, process locks, sandbox limits, and browser-side validation.',
      deps:['owners','navigation','ipc'], skill:'Threat modeling and security boundaries',
      lead:'A renderer contains complicated code processing attacker-controlled HTML, CSS, images, and JavaScript. Chromium therefore cannot make every security decision inside the renderer. This chapter follows the documented compromised-renderer threat model: separate sites, restrict the process, and validate requests at privileged boundaries.',
      flow:['Untrusted page','Renderer process','Restricted capability','Browser validation','Protected resource'],
      sections:[
        ['Start with the compromised-renderer assumption',`<p><code>docs/security/compromised-renderers.md</code> asks what happens if an attacker runs native code inside a renderer sandbox. That attacker can forge messages and bypass checks implemented only in the renderer. The browser and service processes must therefore treat renderer messages as untrusted requests, even when the corresponding web API usually performs checks in Blink.</p><p>This assumption changes how you read an IPC method. Find what resource it can affect, what sender is allowed to call it, and which fact authorizes the operation. If the only answer is “Blink checked first,” the boundary is incomplete under this threat model. The document also lists known gaps, so do not present a platform or site-isolation policy as universal.</p>`],
        ['Origin is authority; URL is often just an address',`<p>A URL identifies a resource, but it may fail to describe the active document’s security origin. <code>about:blank</code> can inherit an origin; a sandboxed frame can have an opaque origin despite an ordinary-looking URL; <code>blob:</code> adds another special case. <code>docs/security/origin-vs-url.md</code> recommends browser-side <code>RenderFrameHost::GetLastCommittedOrigin()</code> for security decisions rather than reconstructing an origin from a displayed or committed URL.</p><p>When reviewing a permission check, write down the precise type of every value: <code>GURL</code>, <code>url::Origin</code>, site, process lock, or visible URL. If code converts between them, ask what information is lost. A type mismatch here is often a threat-model error, not merely a parsing bug.</p>`],
        ['Site isolation and the sandbox solve different problems',`<p>Site isolation places mutually untrusted web content in separate renderer processes under applicable policy. The sandbox restricts what a renderer process can do to the operating system. Neither alone decides whether a particular renderer is allowed to request another origin’s cookie, storage, or network response. That still requires browser-side checks and carefully scoped capabilities.</p><p>The Windows sandbox design describes a browser broker that launches restricted targets and performs some allowed operations on their behalf. Other platforms use different OS mechanisms, as <code>docs/security/process-sandboxes-by-platform.md</code> records. Draw three boundaries for any feature: between origins or sites, between processes, and between a sandboxed process and privileged OS resources. A failure at one boundary should not be hidden by success at another.</p>`],
        ['Bind capabilities to browser-known context',`<p>The compromised-renderer document gives concrete examples. Browser-created <code>URLLoaderFactory</code> objects can carry origin locks. Storage interfaces can be bound using <code>RenderFrameHost::GetLastCommittedOrigin()</code>, and incoming requests can be checked against <code>CanAccessDataForOrigin</code>. The general pattern is to grant a narrow capability based on browser-known state, then validate the operation at use.</p><p>A capability can outlive the state that justified it if ownership is wrong. For each receiver or factory, trace creation, scope, revocation, and teardown. Ask what happens when the frame navigates, is put in BFCache, or its renderer crashes. These are security questions as much as lifecycle questions.</p>`],
        ['Typed IPC does not imply authorized IPC',`<p>Mojo bindings reject malformed messages, but a well-formed request may still name another origin or ask for a privileged action. <code>docs/security/mojo.md</code> recommends simple interfaces, explicit trust documentation, validation of privilege-presuming data, and keeping policy in the browser. The receiver is where a process boundary becomes an authority boundary.</p><p>Consider a fictional <code>OpenFile(path)</code> request from a renderer. The path string might be syntactically valid, yet unrestricted access would defeat the sandbox. A safer interface could pass a browser-issued token for a file the user selected, with the browser resolving that token. This example is a teaching analogy, not a claim that Chromium exposes that exact API.</p>`],
        ['Test the boundary, not just the happy path',`<p>A normal page is often unable to send a forbidden IPC through its public API, so an ordinary web test may never exercise the browser’s defense. Look for tests that construct an invalid request, use a bad-message path, or simulate a compromised renderer. Read the test’s expected outcome: rejection, disconnection, process termination, or a returned error are different contracts.</p><p>For a source review, make a small threat table with attacker-controlled field, claimed authority, browser-known authority, validation method, and failure result. This is more useful than writing “secure because sandboxed.” If you cannot find the browser-side check, mark the claim unresolved and inspect the binder and implementation at the pinned revision.</p>`],
        ['Where the model has limits',`<p>The checked-in threat-model document explicitly calls out gaps, platform differences, and partially implemented protections. Security documentation also changes as attack techniques and mitigations evolve. This chapter teaches the reasoning pattern supported by these sources; it does not certify every current feature or platform configuration.</p><p>When a design relies on a particular guarantee, state the platform, process type, site relationship, and source revision. Then find a test or runtime observation for that exact case. Precision about scope is itself a security skill.</p>`]
      ],
      quiz:['A renderer supplies a URL that appears to belong to example.com. Can the browser use it as proof of that frame’s origin?', 'No. The frame can have an inherited or opaque origin, and a compromised renderer can forge a field. Use browser-known committed origin or validate the claim against it.'],
      lab:{title:'Audit one privileged browser endpoint',aim:'Practice the compromised-renderer threat model on real source.',prediction:'A secure endpoint uses browser-held frame or process context to authorize an operation.',steps:['Read docs/security/compromised-renderers.md and docs/security/origin-vs-url.md.','Choose one storage or network interface named there.','Find the binder, receiver, trusted context, and validation call in pinned source.','Write a forged request the renderer might attempt and predict the browser response; check a related test.'],observation:'The authorization may depend on a committed origin or process lock absent from the message itself.',conclusion:'Process separation limits damage only when privileged services enforce their own policy.',follow:'How does the endpoint change when its frame navigates or enters BFCache?'},
      files:[source('docs/security/compromised-renderers.md','Threat model and concrete protections'),source('docs/security/origin-vs-url.md','Security identity types'),source('docs/security/mojo.md','IPC validation rules'),source('docs/design/sandbox.md','Broker and target model'),source('docs/security/process-sandboxes-by-platform.md','Platform differences'),source('content/browser/child_process_security_policy_impl.cc','Process and origin authorization','src',false)]
    },
    storage: {
      id:'storage', part:'Web APIs and memory', title:'How Chromium keeps page and profile state',
      deck:'Separate document lifetime, cached pages, origin-scoped storage, and profile files.',
      deps:['navigation','security','lifetime'], skill:'State ownership and persistence',
      lead:'“State survives navigation” is too vague to debug. A JavaScript object, a document retained in the back-forward cache, an installed service worker, origin storage, and a profile database have different owners and lifetimes. This chapter gives a method for identifying which kind of state you are looking at.',
      flow:['Document state','BFCache possibility','Origin-scoped service/storage','Profile data','Version migration'],
      sections:[
        ['Make a lifetime table before searching code',`<p>For every piece of state, record its key, owner, storage medium, and deletion condition. A DOM node belongs to a document in a renderer. A BFCache entry may retain that whole document temporarily. A service-worker registration can outlive a tab, while its running worker thread need not. A cookie or site database belongs to a browser-managed storage partition. Bookmarks and history are profile data. These categories overlap in user experience but not in implementation.</p><p>Try the question “Why did this value disappear after Back?” You need to know whether the old document was restored, a new document read persistent storage, or a service worker supplied a response. Seeing the same pixels is insufficient to distinguish those cases.</p>`],
        ['Navigation can destroy or retain a document',`<p><code>docs/bfcache.md</code> explains the ordinary path: navigating away discards the DOM and JavaScript context, so a return visit recreates them. BFCache can instead pause and retain the old page, then restore it without parsing from scratch. Retained does not mean active: a page that is frozen must not continue every operation as though it were visible and current.</p><p>This alters the lifecycle assumptions of browser features. A callback posted before navigation may run after restoration or after the page is evicted. Inspect the page’s lifecycle state, not only whether its C++ object remains reachable. The BFCache overview warns that features interacting with pages need to consider non-fully-active states, including privacy implications.</p>`],
        ['A service worker has two lifetimes',`<p>The supplied service-worker security FAQ distinguishes an installed registration from a running worker thread. A registration is origin-scoped state that can remain after a tab closes. The thread can start to handle an event and stop later. Its exact timeout examples are from an older document, so do not use those numbers as current guarantees.</p><p>The registration is durable; the execution worker is temporary. Code handling an event should not assume an in-memory global remains available forever. To investigate a request, ask whether a worker registration exists, whether a worker was running, and whether it actually intercepted that request.</p>`],
        ['Profile files have compatibility obligations',`<p><code>docs/user_data_dir.md</code> identifies the user data directory and profile path; <code>docs/user_data_storage.md</code> defines compatibility policies. Persistent data is read by future browser versions and sometimes by an older version after downgrade. User-created data should not be casually deleted because one build cannot parse it. Cached or recomputable data can have a different fallback.</p><p>The storage policy also prohibits breaking on-disk compatibility after a release branch is created. Browser storage changes need the same care as database migrations. A schema change needs versioning, upgrade and downgrade behavior, failure handling, and tests with old files. The documentation’s milestone-count example is dated and should not be treated as the current release cadence.</p>`],
        ['Origin storage is a security boundary',`<p>The compromised-renderer threat model says a renderer must not read or write another site’s web storage. It cites <code>StoragePartitionImpl::OpenLocalStorage</code> and browser-side origin checks as examples. Do not assume that a storage API’s JavaScript wrapper is the only gate. Trace the renderer IPC, browser-side receiver, partition or origin key, and underlying data service.</p><p>When comparing two pages, include origin, storage partition, browsing mode, and profile. The same URL opened under different profiles need not see the same data. A visually similar URL with a sandboxed opaque origin may also have a different authority. The origin-versus-URL distinction matters here because it changes which storage a page may access.</p>`],
        ['A profile is not a process',`<p>The profile directory holds data that outlives process crashes and browser restarts. A renderer process may be reused, replaced, or killed without deleting that data. Conversely, a renderer may have a live object that has not yet been written to disk. Ask where the authoritative state resides before diagnosing loss or duplication.</p><p>For a controlled experiment, run Chromium with a temporary <code>--user-data-dir</code>, set a harmless site value, close the browser, and reopen that profile. Compare with a second temporary profile. Use DevTools Application storage and <code>chrome://version</code> to confirm the active profile path. Do not edit a real profile’s files while the browser is running.</p>`],
        ['Design changes as state transitions',`<p>When adding a feature, draw transitions: created, active, frozen, restored, evicted, destroyed, persisted, migrated. Decide what work is allowed in each state and how pending callbacks are cancelled or resumed. Then read one test for every transition that could violate an invariant. This is more precise than adding an “on navigation” callback and hoping it covers every lifecycle path.</p><p>Keep object reachability, document validity, and disk durability separate when you review the design. Memory safety addresses only the first. A correct browser feature must also respect document authority and preserve the user’s persistent data across versions.</p>`]
      ],
      quiz:['A page returns instantly when you press Back. Did Chromium definitely read its data from disk?', 'No. BFCache may have retained the old document and JavaScript context. Check lifecycle events or a trace before attributing the result to storage or HTTP cache.'],
      lab:{title:'Compare three kinds of page state',aim:'Observe document, origin, and profile lifetimes separately.',prediction:'A reload, BFCache restore, and new profile will preserve different subsets of state.',steps:['Read docs/bfcache.md, docs/user_data_dir.md, and docs/user_data_storage.md.','In a temporary profile, make a page store a counter in a JavaScript variable and localStorage.','Navigate away and Back, then reload, then open the same page in a second temporary profile.','Record the values and lifecycle events; identify which state source explains each observation.'],observation:'A retained document can preserve an in-memory value; persistent origin storage can survive reload; profiles isolate disk state.',conclusion:'The phrase “saved state” hides several owners and lifetimes.',follow:'What security check binds a storage IPC to the frame’s committed origin?'},
      files:[source('docs/bfcache.md','Retained-document lifecycle'),source('docs/user_data_dir.md','Profile and user data path'),source('docs/user_data_storage.md','Versioned file compatibility'),source('docs/security/service-worker-security-faq.md','Historical service-worker lifecycle guidance'),source('docs/security/compromised-renderers.md','Origin storage protection'),source('content/browser/storage_partition_impl.cc','Browser storage partition','src',false),source('content/browser/renderer_host/back_forward_cache_impl.cc','BFCache decisions','src',false)]
    },
    diagnostics: {
      id:'diagnostics', part:'Engineering practice', title:'How to debug and measure Chromium',
      deck:'Use traces, profiles, memory evidence, and controlled comparisons.',
      deps:['time','pixels','factory','storage'], skill:'Observation and performance reasoning',
      lead:'A source explanation is a hypothesis about runtime behavior. To improve it, measure a specific path, compare runs, and identify the earliest stage where expectation and observation diverge. Chromium’s tracing, profiling, memory, and CI documents provide the tools and their limits.',
      flow:['State a symptom','Predict a cause','Collect focused evidence','Compare baseline','Locate first divergence','Test a fix'],
      sections:[
        ['Turn a symptom into a falsifiable question',`<p>“The page is slow” does not identify a subsystem. Ask whether the delay is before response headers, before navigation commit, in a main-thread task, during style/layout, in raster, or in final display. Predict one observation for each plausible cause. For example, a long script task should occupy the renderer main thread; a slow network response should delay bytes before parsing. This step determines which trace categories or profile to collect.</p><p>Choose one reproducible action and one metric. Record hardware, OS, build revision, command-line flags, profile state, and page content. Chromium behavior varies with all of them. A measurement without its conditions is difficult to compare and easy to overinterpret.</p>`],
        ['A trace shows temporal relationships',`<p><code>docs/trace_events.md</code> recommends focused Perfetto trace categories and modern <code>TRACE_EVENT</code> macros. A trace can show when a task ran, when a frame update began, and how work crossed threads or processes. It does not by itself prove why code chose a branch. Pair the timeline with source and test behavior.</p><p>For an asynchronous operation, use a flow or suitable track to connect start and completion. Label process, thread, and identifier. Be careful with a long asynchronous event: it represents logical duration, not continuous CPU use. A span crossing 100 ms may include waiting, another process, and no work on the thread where it began.</p>`],
        ['Profiles answer where CPU time went',`<p>A sampling profile collects stacks at intervals and estimates where CPU execution time was spent. A trace explains sequence and latency; a profile often better identifies hot functions. <code>docs/profiling.md</code> describes tools such as <code>perf</code> and platform profilers. Select the process and time window relevant to the symptom; profiling the browser process will miss a renderer’s expensive JavaScript or layout work.</p><p>Samples can be distorted by inlining, missing symbols, warmup, and low counts. A single hot frame need not be the root cause; it may be a necessary worker doing extra work because of an earlier invalidation. Read callers and compare a baseline before changing the hot function.</p>`],
        ['Memory numbers are not interchangeable',`<p><code>docs/memory/key_concepts.md</code> distinguishes virtual address space, committed memory, resident pages, and platform-specific accounting. A renderer’s visible working set is not the same as total system commitment. Shared libraries and GPU resources further complicate “how much memory Chromium uses.” Choose the metric that matches the failure mechanism.</p><p><code>docs/memory/oom.md</code> lists several OOM causes: system pressure, commit limits, virtual-address exhaustion, sandbox limits, and an excessive single allocation. The crashing allocation may be where pressure became fatal, not where the waste began. For a suspected leak, compare snapshots over the same workload, separate retained objects from allocator free lists, and inspect whether navigation or garbage collection changed the baseline.</p>`],
        ['Performance changes need controlled comparisons',`<p>Chromium’s performance documentation distinguishes local experiments, lab benchmarks, and end-user metrics. A local before/after run gives quick feedback but can be noisy; automated benchmarks improve repeatability; user metrics reflect a broader population but make causal attribution harder. Choose the evidence appropriate to the claim you want to make.</p><p>Change one variable when possible. Repeat runs, retain distributions rather than one best time, and compare the same hardware and build configuration. If a change improves average frame time but creates rare long tasks, a single mean hides an important regression. Record both the intended benefit and a plausible cost before measuring.</p>`],
        ['Follow one frame from task to display',`<p>Use the renderer chapter’s task and rendering stages as a diagnostic map. A JavaScript mutation may invalidate style. Style may change geometry and require layout. Paint creates display items; cc commits compositor state; raster produces pixels; Viz aggregates and draws. If the screen is stale, find the first stage whose new state is absent. Do not call every visual delay “paint.”</p><p>For a frame investigation, record timestamps for input, task execution, style/layout, paint, commit, raster, activation, and display where available. If one stage is missing, inspect why it was not scheduled. If all stages occurred but the result is wrong, inspect state and ownership rather than only timing.</p>`],
        ['Test the explanation, then preserve it',`<p>Once a trace supports a cause, make a small change or focused test that should alter one observable. For example, remove one expensive style invalidation and predict the reduction in layout work; or delay one Mojo reply and predict which UI state remains pending. Compare before and after. If the result differs, revise the model instead of defending the initial story.</p><p>Write a short investigation record: symptom, environment, hypothesis, evidence, source paths, test or experiment, alternative explanation, and remaining question. This format makes the work reviewable and trains the habit the book aims to teach: replace confident guesses with checked causal models.</p>`]
      ],
      quiz:['A trace shows a 200 ms asynchronous event. Did one thread use 200 ms of CPU?', 'Not necessarily. The span may include queueing and waiting. Inspect thread slices or a CPU profile before claiming CPU cost.'],
      lab:{title:'Measure one reproducible frame delay',aim:'Connect a user-visible delay to a task, rendering stage, or wait.',prediction:'A deliberately long script task will block renderer main-thread work and delay the next visual update.',steps:['Read docs/trace_events.md and docs/how_cc_works.md.','Use a small local page that changes a style property after a deliberately long script task.','Capture a focused trace or performance recording and mark input, task, frame update, and display.','Repeat without the long task and compare the same timestamps; inspect the responsible source stage.'],observation:'The task occupies main-thread time; later rendering work moves when the task is removed.',conclusion:'A causal performance claim needs a controlled difference and a timeline.',follow:'Would a compositor-only property change alter which thread is on the critical path?'},
      files:[source('docs/trace_events.md','Trace event guidance'),source('docs/profiling.md','CPU profiling'),source('docs/memory/key_concepts.md','Memory accounting'),source('docs/memory/oom.md','Out-of-memory diagnosis'),source('docs/speed/how_does_chrome_measure_performance.md','Measurement contexts'),source('docs/how_cc_works.md','Frame production stages')]
    },
    journey: {
      id:'journey', part:'Engineering practice', title:'A page load from click to pixels',
      deck:'Connect process, network, security, parser, script, compositor, and trace evidence.',
      deps:['navigation','network','pixels','diagnostics'], skill:'End-to-end system reasoning',
      lead:'A complex system becomes understandable when you can follow one event across its boundaries. This worked case starts when a user follows a link and ends when the new page becomes visible. It does not assume every page uses the same fast path; each stage states the decision that can change the route.',
      flow:['Input','Navigation','Response and commit','Parse and script','Style and paint','Compositor and display'],
      sections:[
        ['Write the question before opening the tree',`<p>Suppose the user clicks a cross-site link and reports: “The address bar changed, but the old page stayed visible for a moment; then the new page appeared without its stylesheet.” There are at least two independent symptoms. The visible URL may reflect a pending navigation before the new document commits. The missing stylesheet is a later subresource or rendering problem. Treating both as “slow network” would merge distinct states.</p><p>Before searching, make a timeline with six slots: input handled; navigation requested; response headers accepted; document committed; stylesheet loaded and style calculated; compositor frame displayed. Predict which process owns each slot and how you would observe it. You will revise the timeline after reading source. That revision is the point of the exercise.</p>`],
        ['Input becomes a request to navigate',`<p>Input can begin in compositor or renderer paths, depending on the event and page. A link click commonly reaches renderer-side document behavior, which asks the browser to navigate. The browser holds the authoritative <code>NavigationRequest</code> for a cross-document navigation. <code>docs/navigation.md</code> describes the stages; <code>docs/frame_trees.md</code> explains which browser frame object is being replaced.</p><p>Find the specific entry point for your test page rather than treating “click” as one universal call path. Record the frame ID, initiator origin, target URL, and whether the navigation is browser- or renderer-initiated. At this point the old document can still be alive. The browser may display a pending URL without granting it the old document’s authority.</p>`],
        ['The response chooses the next path',`<p>Before ordinary network I/O, the request may be intercepted by a service worker, internal loader, or cache. A server may redirect, return no content, trigger a download, or return a document. The navigation chapter’s response rules determine whether Chromium keeps the old document, changes process, or commits an error page. The browser also applies response-derived policy such as an opaque origin from sandboxing.</p><p>For the case at hand, check response status and headers of the document request first. If it is 204, your expected “new page” should never commit. If it is a normal HTML response, find the selected <code>RenderFrameHost</code> and the commit acknowledgement. A process change is not necessarily a tab change; it follows site and isolation policy.</p>`],
        ['Commit changes authority before every pixel is ready',`<p>At authoritative commit, the browser updates its notion of the current document. Blink in the chosen renderer creates the new document and begins loading response bytes. Acknowledging commit does not imply that the parser has finished, JavaScript has run, or a frame is visible. <code>docs/navigation.md</code> deliberately separates navigation from loading for this reason.</p><p>Draw a vertical line at commit. To its left, ask why the old page is still authoritative. To its right, ask which work remains: body bytes, parser tokens, resource discovery, style, layout, paint, raster. If your trace shows the new committed origin but the old visual frame, that can be a normal transient state. A long or permanent mismatch needs a stage-specific explanation.</p>`],
        ['The parser creates objects and can run code',`<p>HTML bytes become tokens; tree-building uses insertion modes; construction sites create elements. The generated HTML name tables connect input strings to internal IDs and classes. A custom element constructor may run script synchronously during construction, so parser work is not a pure conversion of text into a tree. The bindings layer then connects page JavaScript through V8 wrappers to Blink objects.</p><p>In the example, inspect when the stylesheet link is discovered and what resource request it creates. If JavaScript modifies that link, the final request may differ from the literal HTML. A parser or binding call path tells you what was requested; a network trace tells you whether it returned; a style trace tells you whether its rules affected the document. Each source answers a different part of the claim.</p>`],
        ['A stylesheet can fail at several boundaries',`<p>The renderer may fail to create a request, a factory may reject it, the network service may return an error, cross-origin policy may block the response, the CSS parser may reject content, or the stylesheet may load but not match any element. These cases can look similar in a screenshot. Locate the earliest observed divergence: request absent, bytes absent, rules absent, computed style unchanged, or paint unchanged.</p><p>For a controlled test, first use a same-origin stylesheet with an obvious color rule, then make one change at a time: wrong URL, blocked cross-origin response, invalid CSS, unmatched selector. Record DevTools Network status, console output, computed style, and a trace. The differing observations train you to classify failures by stage instead of by appearance.</p>`],
        ['New visual state travels through more than the DOM',`<p>A successful style calculation may invalidate layout if geometry changes, or only paint if a color changes. Paint records drawing commands and property state; the compositor commits a new tree, rasterizes needed content, and Viz aggregates the compositor frame for display. The old active tree can remain draw-ready while a new pending tree is prepared. This explains why “the DOM changed” and “the screen changed” are separate events.</p><p>Follow <code>docs/how_cc_works.md</code> and the rendering chapter. For the stylesheet example, predict which rendering stage is first affected. Then inspect computed style and frame events. If the style is correct but pixels are stale, focus on paint/commit/display; if computed style is wrong, do not begin with Viz.</p>`],
        ['Overlay ownership, time, and failure',`<p>Annotate the timeline with process and thread lanes: browser navigation and policy; renderer main-thread parsing, script, style and layout; compositor work; Viz display. Add IPC edges for navigation commit, loader capabilities, and compositor frames. A failure in one lane may leave another lane functioning. A renderer crash loses its DOM; the browser retains its frame-tree and navigation management. A network error after commit can leave an error or partial page, depending on the stage.</p><div class="model-table-wrap"><table class="model-table"><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Primary owner</th><th>What to verify</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Navigation</td><td>Browser process</td><td>Requested versus committed document</td></tr><tr><td>Response</td><td>Loader and browser policy</td><td>Source, headers, and allowed bytes</td></tr><tr><td>Document</td><td>Renderer main thread</td><td>Parser, script, style, and layout state</td></tr><tr><td>Frame</td><td>Compositor and Viz</td><td>Commit, raster, aggregation, display</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Use this table as a working model and verify the actual call graph in source. Whenever an edge is uncertain, attach an exact source search, test, or trace event that could confirm it. Separating known edges from hypotheses makes the model useful under review.</p>`],
        ['Prove the story with one small experiment',`<p>Build a local page with a link and external stylesheet. Capture a baseline trace and network log. Change only the stylesheet URL to return an error; then restore it and add a deliberate long renderer task. Predict how each change affects request status, commit timing, style calculation, and display. The two manipulations should produce different signatures even if both leave the user waiting for the right pixels.</p><p>Write the result as a short incident report: user symptom, timeline, source paths, observations, rejected hypotheses, and a remaining uncertainty. If you cannot run Chromium, provide the exact trace setup and predicted difference. An honest unrun experiment is better than a fabricated observation.</p>`]
      ],
      quiz:['A document has committed and its DOM contains a styled element, but the screen still shows old pixels. Which evidence should you collect next?', 'Check computed style and then the paint, compositor commit, raster, and display stages. A DOM observation alone does not locate the visual delay.'],
      lab:{title:'Produce a cross-process page-load timeline',aim:'Combine the book’s models into a checked explanation of one navigation.',prediction:'The new document can commit before all subresources and visual stages complete.',steps:['Create two simple local pages; link from one to the other, and give the target an external stylesheet.','Record browser and renderer process IDs, the document and stylesheet requests, and a performance trace.','Mark response headers, commit, parser/resource discovery, style/layout, paint, compositor commit, and display.','Break the stylesheet URL, then restore it and add a long task. Compare both runs with the baseline.','Cite one source path and one observed event for each boundary; mark any unverified edge.'],observation:'The stylesheet failure and main-thread delay should have different earliest divergences.',conclusion:'An end-to-end explanation is a chain of independently checkable state transitions.',follow:'Repeat the investigation with a service worker or a cross-site iframe and identify which edges change.'},
      files:[source('docs/navigation.md','Request, commit, and loading'),source('docs/frame_trees.md','Frame ownership'),source('docs/security/compromised-renderers.md','Network and process authority'),source('docs/mojo_and_services.md','IPC path'),source('docs/how_cc_works.md','Compositor path'),source('docs/trace_events.md','Timeline instrumentation')]
    }
  };
}
